1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Welcome to HealthTwin, a research prototype for exploring
short-horizon glucose-event prediction and explanation.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This walkthrough follows one synthetic demo patient through
the dashboard, patient context, twin, prediction, and reports.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The interface is designed to connect several data views
around the same selected patient and time window.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
This is not a medical device, clinical record,
or source of treatment recommendations.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,000
All displayed data are synthetic, and model outputs
should be interpreted as research demonstrations only.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:02,000
We begin on Home with patient P0001 selected.
The header also shows the application’s mock-data status.

7
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The EHR Profile summarizes context such as age,
BMI, HbA1c, blood pressure, and medication.

8
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:26,000
These values help orient the selected synthetic record;
they are not a substitute for a medical history.

9
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Live Wearables presents the simulated glucose, heart-rate,
heart-rate-variability, activity, sleep, and stress indicators.

10
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The word simulated matters: these cards are not
connected to a person’s wearable device.

11
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Data Fusion sketches how EHR and wearable inputs
are brought together for a model-window view.

12
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The central twin visualization marks model-derived indicators;
it does not depict measured anatomy or organ function.

13
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Below it, the event definition names the displayed
spike threshold and the two-hour prediction horizon.

14
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The chart places observed glucose beside a forecast
and marks the configured display threshold.

15
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:50,000
In this captured window, the interface reports a
194 milligram-per-deciliter latest glucose value.

16
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:02,000
The forecast and risk band are model outputs
for this synthetic example, not a diagnosis.

17
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Next, I’ll move the shared time cursor to
show how the selected window anchors the dashboard.

18
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:26,000
The time slider and play control change the
selected point used across the app’s views.

19
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Opening alerts reveals application notifications associated with
the current demonstration state.

20
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:50,000
An alert here is a model or interface signal,
not a clinical decision rule.

21
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:02,000
I’ll return to the navigation and open Patient Profile,
keeping the same synthetic patient selected.

22
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:14,000
The profile gathers record-level context and selected-window
information into a focused page.

23
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Age, sex, BMI, HbA1c, medication, and diabetes
duration provide descriptive context for this example.

24
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The glucose and prediction sections connect that context
to a specific point in the time series.

25
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:50,000
When recording, pause on each section long enough
for its labels and values to be read.

26
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:02,000
The profile is still a synthetic demonstration record;
no real patient information is shown.

27
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:14,000
I’ll now open Digital Twin to inspect the
model’s summary for the selected window.

28
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:26,000
The body graphic is an explanatory interface, not
an anatomical scan or physiological measurement.

29
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:38,000
The current model-window panel reports a glucose-spike prediction
for the next two hours.

30
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:50,000
The displayed probability belongs to this selected synthetic
window and should not guide care.

31
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Window statistics summarize the recent values, including average,
variability, minimum, and maximum glucose.

32
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Patient context repeats selected EHR fields so viewers
can compare them with the model summary.

33
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:26,000
The factor groups show attribution assigned by the
model for this window.

34
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Attribution describes which inputs influenced this output;
it does not establish cause and effect.

35
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:50,000
The page also states that the twin is
research-only and does not estimate hidden physiology.

36
00:06:50,000 --> 00:07:02,000
From here, I’ll open Predictions to inspect the
score, explanation, and model card together.

37
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:14,000
The prediction page identifies the event definition and
the selected patient window.

38
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Its factor contribution list is normalized to a
complete share of the displayed model attribution.

39
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Recent glucose trend is the largest contribution in
this captured example, followed by other input groups.

40
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:50,000
The “Why now” explanation points to the displayed
threshold and the recent glucose slope.

41
00:07:50,000 --> 00:08:02,000
These are explanatory model signals, not validated
clinical criteria or causal conclusions.

42
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Window statistics show the mean, spread, minimum, maximum,
last value, and recent slope.

43
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:26,000
The model card lists AUROC, AUPRC, F1,
and calibration error from a stored snapshot.

44
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Confidence intervals are unavailable, so these point estimates
should not be read as precise guarantees.

45
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:50,000
The page also warns that stored metrics and
currently processed labels need reconciliation.

46
00:08:50,000 --> 00:09:02,000
That provenance caveat is important context, not
fine print to skip over.

47
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Now I’ll open What-If Simulator and wait
for its baseline scenarios to load.

48
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:26,000
The page compares a current trajectory with alternative
synthetic scenarios over the same two-hour window.

49
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:38,000
A warning states that stored monotonicity checks fail;
scenario changes may move in unexpected directions.

50
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:50,000
I’ll select a meal preset to change the
carbohydrate input for a custom scenario.

51
00:09:50,000 --> 00:10:02,000
The controls also include activity, sleep quality,
medication adherence, stress, and glucose offset.

52
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:14,000
These are model inputs for an experiment, not
instructions for changing someone’s behavior or treatment.

53
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:26,000
After choosing an input, I’ll run the custom
scenario and wait for the response.

54
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:38,000
The chart updates to compare the available trajectories
against the displayed glucose threshold.

55
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:50,000
The outcome cards summarize peak glucose, spike probability,
approximate time above threshold, and probability change.

56
00:10:50,000 --> 00:11:02,000
A difference in this scenario is a model
comparison, not evidence of an intervention effect.

57
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:14,000
The simulator is useful for exploring model behavior
and the sensitivity of its displayed outputs.

58
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Its results can be counterintuitive, and the interface
explicitly says they are not causal advice.

59
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:38,000
I’ll leave the result visible briefly, then
move to the evaluation report.

60
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Reports begins with a summary of stored
classification metrics and their provenance caveat.

61
00:11:50,000 --> 00:12:02,000
The page says confidence intervals are unavailable
for the displayed metric snapshot.

62
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Its comparison table includes the fusion model
and several baseline or ablation models.

63
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:26,000
In this snapshot, the XGBoost baseline scores above
the full fusion model on AUROC.

64
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:38,000
That result is shown honestly; model complexity does
not guarantee better performance on synthetic data.

65
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:50,000
The robustness section reports failed checks for
some expected intervention directions.

66
00:12:50,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Those failures reinforce why simulator changes should not
be presented as guaranteed risk reduction.

67
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:14,000
If the Horizons view is available, I’ll open
it and show how metrics vary by horizon.

68
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:26,000
I’ll scroll through the report at a
steady pace so the comparison labels remain readable.

69
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Stored evaluation figures are useful for reviewing this
prototype, but are not independently reproduced here.

70
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:50,000
The report’s caveats are part of the
result and belong in the demo.

71
00:13:50,000 --> 00:14:02,000
Next I’ll open Settings and review the
preferences available in this local application.

72
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Glucose units, alert threshold, prediction horizon, data-source
label, and theme are configurable here.

73
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:26,000
The interface clarifies that changing the displayed horizon
does not change the model’s trained target.

74
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:38,000
The privacy log is stored locally and
is not a clinical consent workflow.

75
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:50,000
I’ll return to Home for a final
look at the complete dashboard.

76
00:14:50,000 --> 00:15:02,000
HealthTwin brings patient context, simulated streams, forecasts,
and model explanations into one interface.

77
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Its value in this demo is transparent exploration,
including visible limitations and evaluation caveats.

78
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:26,000
It is a research prototype using synthetic data,
not a clinical decision-support product.

79
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Model outputs are not medical advice; no treatment
decisions should be made from this demonstration.

80
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:50,000
Thank you for watching this walkthrough of
the HealthTwin website and its current boundaries.

81
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:00,000
This concludes the walkthrough. HealthTwin remains a
research prototype using synthetic data.
